.TH HV_FCOPY_UIO_DAEMON 8 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
hv_fcopy_uio_daemon \- Hyper-V guest file copy daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hv_fcopy_uio_daemon
.RB [ \-n ]
.br
.B hv_fcopy_uio_daemon
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hv_fcopy_uio_daemon
implements the guest side of the Hyper-V file copy service, through which
the host copies files into a running Linux guest.
Unlike
.BR \%hv_kvp_daemon (8)
and
.BR \%hv_vss_daemon (8),
it talks to the host directly over the file copy VMBus channel, which
the kernel exposes through the Userspace I/O (UIO) framework.
.PP
The daemon expects the file copy device at
.IR \%/sys/\:bus/\:vmbus/\:devices/\:eb765408\-105f\-49b6\-b4aa\-c123b64d17d4/ ,
referred to below as
.IR device .
At startup it detaches from the terminal with
.BR daemon (3)
unless
.B \-n
is given; the working directory is not changed, and standard input,
standard output and standard error are redirected to
.IR /dev/null .
It then:
.IP \(bu 3
takes half the size of the first
.IR device /channels/*/ring
file it finds as the size of each of the channel's two ring buffers,
retrying once after 100 milliseconds if none is found;
.IP \(bu 3
takes the name of the UIO device from the first entry in
.IR device /uio/,
for example
.BR uio0 ,
and opens the corresponding device node, for example
.IR /dev/uio0 ;
.IP \(bu 3
maps the ring buffers into memory.
.PP
It then waits for interrupts from the host and processes each message
it receives, replying to the host through the ring buffer:
.TP
Version negotiation
The daemon accepts file copy service version 1.1.
.TP
Start of a copy
The host sends a destination directory and a file name.
If a directory in the destination path, up to the path's last slash,
does not exist, the daemon creates it with mode 0755 when the host has
requested this, and otherwise fails the copy.
If the file already exists, the copy fails unless the host has
requested that existing files be overwritten.
The file is then created, or truncated, with mode 0744 as modified by
the process umask.
.TP
Data
The data is written at the offset the host specifies.
If the file system is full, the host is told that the disk is full.
.TP
End of a copy
The file is closed.
.PP
Any other file copy request fails.
The daemon handles one file at a time.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-daemon
Stay in the foreground; do not detach from the terminal.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary to standard error and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hv_fcopy_uio_daemon
exits with status 234 (the low byte of
.BR \-EINVAL )
after
.B \-\-help
or an invalid option, and also if
.BR daemon (3)
fails.
Otherwise it runs until an error occurs, such as failing to determine
the ring buffer size, to open or map the UIO device, or to read from
it.
When the daemon detaches, the invoking process exits with status 0 as
soon as the background process has been created; later errors are
reported only in the system log.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I \%/sys/\:bus/\:vmbus/\:devices/\:eb765408\-105f\-49b6\-b4aa\-c123b64d17d4/
The file copy VMBus device.
The daemon looks for the device only under this instance GUID.
It reads the size of the
.I channels/*/ring
files and the name of the UIO device in
.I uio/
from this directory.
.TP
.IR /dev/uio N
UIO device node through which the daemon receives interrupts, signals
the host, and maps the ring buffers.
.SH NOTES
The daemon logs through
.BR syslog (3)
with the identifier
.B \%HV_UIO_FCOPY
and the facility
.BR \%LOG_USER .
.PP
The daemon works only in a Linux guest running on Hyper-V, and only
when the file copy device is bound to the kernel's generic VMBus UIO
driver, uio_hv_generic
.RB \%( CONFIG_UIO_HV_GENERIC ).
That driver has no built-in device IDs, so it must be told which
devices to bind to.
The comment at the top of
.I \%drivers/\:uio/\:uio_hv_generic.c
in the Linux source describes doing this by writing the device's class
GUID to
.IR \%/sys/\:bus/\:vmbus/\:drivers/\:uio_hv_generic/\:new_id .
The class GUID of the file copy device is
.BR \%34d14be3\-dee4\-41c8\-9ae7\-6b174977c192 .
.PP
The destination directory and file name come from the host, and the
daemon does not restrict where it writes: any path that the daemon's
privileges allow can be created or, if the host requests it,
overwritten.
.SH EXAMPLES
Bind the file copy device to uio_hv_generic, then start the daemon:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# echo 34d14be3\-dee4\-41c8\-9ae7\-6b174977c192 \e
    > /sys/bus/vmbus/drivers/uio_hv_generic/new_id
# hv_fcopy_uio_daemon
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR \%hv_kvp_daemon (8),
.BR \%hv_vss_daemon (8),
.BR lsvmbus (1),
.BR daemon (3),
.BR syslog (3)
.PP
.I \%Documentation/\:virt/\:hyperv/
in the Linux source.
